WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.780
- If you went back in time
a hundred years or so,

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.950
you'd see a lot of people doing jobs

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.660
that don't exist anymore.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.260
Since most homes didn't
have refrigeration,

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:12.800
milkman deliver dairy to
your doorstep every morning

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.450
and had an affair with your wife

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.530
if fifties comedy is accurate.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.360
Elevators were driven like street cars,

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.140
which meant elevator
operators had to be trained

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.640
to ensure a smooth and
precise ride for passengers.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.250
Phone lines weren't automated

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.390
so switchboard operators were needed

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:30.240
to connect you to your party.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.640
Even the bowling alley had
a team of manual pin setters

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.960
to prepare your next frame.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.710
- These jobs may seem
quaint or even absurd

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:39.740
by today's standards,

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.790
but don't feel too superior.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.220
Perhaps a hundred years from now,

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:44.730
someone will look back at your job

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.190
and wonder why you were necessary.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.857
(bright music)

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.970
- Fear of technological
unemployment is nothing new.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:02.630
As far back as 350 BC,
Aristotle contemplated

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:05.960
the idea of machines
performing human labor.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.670
During the Industrial Revolution,

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:09.630
textile workers known as Luddites

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.730
destroy the new fangle
machinery at the factory.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:16.200
And the steel-driving man
of folklore, John Henry

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.650
literally killed himself

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.600
competing against a steam-powered machine.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.650
- In 1930, the famed
economist, John Maynard Keynes

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.340
took a more optimistic
view of the problem.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.450
Predicting that automation and technology

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:32.090
would soon allow people to
work only 15 hours a week.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.450
That would have been nice.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.970
Of course, today we know

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.400
that neither Keynes nor the
Luddites had it quite right.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.200
Technology has made us
far more productive,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.380
but it seems there's still
plenty of work to be done.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.710
- Most economists argue that technology

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:50.390
doesn't so much eliminate
jobs as change them,

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:53.060
that the number of tasks rendered obsolete

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.340
are more than made up for by new ones.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.890
So instead of switchboard operators,

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:01.340
now we have cell phone
repairmen, broadband technicians,

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.660
and mobile app developers.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.350
This may be cold comfort

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:07.580
if you happen to have
spent half a lifetime

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.770
becoming the fastest switchboard
operator in the West.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.660
But if it's any consolation,

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.010
there are plenty of other
workers in the same boat.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:19.140
- Automation has been changing
manufacturing and agriculture

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.520
for some time.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.410
We're used to seeing robots
in factories and on farms,

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.480
but recently, the machines
have been expanding

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.050
their territory.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:32.230
Automatic kiosks are replacing
cashiers and bank tellers.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.470
Fast food companies are experimenting

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.420
with burger-flipping robots.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.000
And one robo-caller can
disrupt as many dinners

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.420
as a whole floor of telemarketers.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.090
Thanks to advancements in
artificial intelligence,

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.640
self-driving vehicles are
quickly becoming a reality.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.810
Putting at risk anyone who
makes a living behind the wheel

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:53.900
of a cab, truck or bus.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.950
Amazon is even implementing
unmanned drones

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.840
to deliver packages.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.850
Basically, any job that
involves repetitive,

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.750
predictable tasks is ripe for automation.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.460
- But the machines aren't just
coming for the blue collars,

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.030
clerical jobs are getting
automated as well.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.280
Insurance underwriters and loan officers

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.870
who research an individual
or company's history

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.230
and calculate a precise level of risk

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.250
are being replaced by algorithms

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:22.570
that can crunch more data
faster and more accurately.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.350
- Purchasing agents who
maintain a company's stock

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:29.050
of merchandise and equipment
are outperformed by computers

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:32.300
that order, track, and
invoice at a higher volume.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:35.590
And lawyers with access
to vast online databases

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:38.980
have less need for
paralegals and secretaries.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:42.150
- Even tax preparers
and financial advisors

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.560
are feeling the heat
from apps and websites

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.790
that analyze a customer's portfolio

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:48.640
and give custom guidance.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.740
- Okay, now this is getting scary.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.960
- So what jobs aren't threatened

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.340
by technology's pitiless advance?

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.250
Well, no job is safe forever,

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:59.750
but if you at least want
to buy yourself some time,

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.820
you can focus on new and
burgeoning industries.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.320
Careers in genetics, video game design,

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:08.210
and social media marketing
have seen strong growth

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:09.450
in recent years.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:12.670
Alternative energy and
sustainability are also on the rise.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:15.240
And it's no coincidence
that there's high demand

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.130
in the fields of robotics
and data science.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:18.963
That's right.

00:04:18.963 --> 00:04:21.560
You can get a good job
teaching robots and algorithms

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.800
to take other people's jobs away.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.120
Kidding, not kidding.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.700
- Another field that seems
to be safe for the time being

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:28.970
is the arts.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:32.040
Some cutting edge software
companies are developing AI

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.470
that can write poems, draw
pictures, compose music,

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.690
even design fashion.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.580
And while they might be impressive

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.560
from a technological standpoint,

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.300
none of them are close to
competing with human artists.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.940
It may sometimes feel like
franchise movies and pop songs

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.260
are written by computers,

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:51.460
but I assure you,

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:54.740
there are real people
behind them, I think.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.620
So, congratulations, poets and musicians!

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:59.960
You can now brag to your
insurance underwriter brother

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.600
that you made a better career decision.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.360
- However, your plumber cousin

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.290
has got the better of both of you.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:10.040
Trades like plumbing, electrical,
and HVAC are well-paid

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.630
and fairly protected from automation.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:15.440
That's because they're often unpredictable

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.930
and machines do not do
well with unpredictability.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.390
These workers are called out
to random homes and businesses,

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:25.810
asked to diagnose and
fix unexpected problems.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.470
Robots have a long way to
go before they can match

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.410
that kind of flexibility.

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:33.070
- But perhaps the most
future-proof jobs in America

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:36.380
are the ones that rely on
interpersonal interactions.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:40.840
Social workers, counselors,
occupational therapists, clergy,

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.380
the more tact and compassion required,

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.770
the safer the job is from
technological replacement.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.980
Even if a computer could understand

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.220
the complexities of human emotion,

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:54.090
it's still couldn't
form the bonds of trust

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.180
and mutual respect that
these workers must forge

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:58.590
with their clients.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.470
- Particularly in demand right now

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:03.920
and for the foreseeable
future, is elderly care.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:05.800
Not only does the profession require

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.930
a distinctly human touch,

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:09.570
America's elderly population

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.980
is quickly reaching historic levels.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:15.070
We may be headed for a severe
labor shortage in this field,

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:18.100
one that robots aren't ready to fill.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.060
- Neither humans nor computers

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:21.950
are too good at predicting the future

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:24.750
so take all of this with a grain of salt.

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:26.400
One thing is certain though,

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.220
technology keeps moving
and not much can stop it.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:32.240
You may think you have a cutting edge job

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.650
but so did the elevator operator once.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.750
- The best thing you can
do to protect yourself

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:39.340
is to pay attention to
innovations in your field

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:41.920
and be ready to learn new skills.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:44.970
Your job may someday get
taken over by a robot,

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:48.240
but someone has to design
that robot and build it

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:51.730
and program it and maintain
it and supervise it.

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:54.490
- Someone already in the
field is in a better position

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:57.670
to take one of those new jobs
when the old one goes the way

00:06:57.670 --> 00:06:59.080
of the milkman.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:00.535
- [Both] And that's our two cents.

00:07:00.535 --> 00:07:02.510
- Quick, before you go,

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:04.830
do you know what you
wanna be when you grow up?

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.050
- No matter your answer,

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:08.810
we think you should check
out "Future of Work,"

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.710
a new six-part docuseries on PBS Voices

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:13.210
hosted by Julia and me

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:16.280
that explores the real
big work-related questions

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:18.320
that Americans are facing today.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:22.140
Everything from debt and the
gig economy to career identity,

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:23.200
and so much more.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:24.033
- Check it out.

00:07:24.033 --> 00:07:26.150
Link in our description
and be sure to tell them

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:27.713
that Two Cents sent you.

